course: dictionary
lesson: 12385
title: Culpability: who is to blame?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Culpability is a formal word meaning being to blame for something wrong or bad. You will meet it in law, in the news and in serious talks about right and wrong. The context helps you: «The judge looked carefully at the evidence before deciding the driver's culpability.» Judges, evidence and deciding are all clues that this is about who is to blame.
explain: Look at the word «deny» in this sentence: «She did not deny culpability; she said sorry at once.» To deny something is to say it is not true, so she did not say the mistake was not hers. The word «sorry» confirms she took the blame.
explain: In ethics lessons, we ask about culpability when someone did not mean any harm: «He broke the vase by accident, so his moral culpability was small.» Moral culpability is blame based on right and wrong. It does not mean someone is blameless, only that they are less to blame.
fact: Culpability means being to blame for something wrong or bad.
fact: Culpability is uncountable: say «the culpability», never «a culpability» or «culpabilities».
fact: Common phrases are «accept culpability», «deny culpability» and «moral culpability».
fact: «Fault» is the simpler, everyday word with a similar meaning; culpability is the stiffer, more formal choice.
fact: Use culpability in law, news and serious discussions, and use fault in everyday talk.
exercise: What does culpability mean? | Being the first to finish a race | *Being to blame for something wrong or bad | Being very tired after a long day | Being happy with a prize
exercise: The boy said, «It was not me.» Which phrase fits best? | accept culpability | *deny culpability | culpability is a prize | moral culpability
exercise: Choose the correct sentence. | *The judge studied his culpability. | The judge studied his a culpability. | The judge studied his culpable. | The judge studied his culpabilities.
test: Which everyday word means nearly the same as culpability? | Brightness | Sleepiness | *Fault | Happiness
test: In ethics lessons, what do we ask about someone who did not mean harm? | What they had for lunch | *How much they are to blame | Where they live | How tall they are
exam: Read this: «The court decided the company's culpability was small.» Culpability here means | a list of prices | a happy feeling | *blame for something wrong | a large building
